
#!/usr/bin/python3
# Generated by Ask CK PyTest Creator for AWPTCM-T44187 — (57) IPv6 - SSH Server
# Standardized ART framework script. Frame is fixed; test logic is filled per step.
#
# ==== OBJECTIVE — expected results this script must demonstrate ====
#   - An SSH server enabled on the DUT and reachable over IPv6 on the standard SSH port,
#   with IPv6 addressing configured on the management/transit interface.
#   - Successful SSH sessions established from an IPv6 client to the DUT using global
#   unicast, link-local (with scope/zone identifier) and, where supported, loopback IPv6
#   addresses.
#   - Authenticated interactive shell over IPv6 with a valid username and password, granting
#   a functional CLI prompt at the expected privilege level.
#   - CLI commands issued over the IPv6 SSH session returning correct, complete and
#   unmangled output, equivalent to the same commands issued over IPv4 SSH or console.
#   - Equivalent SSH server behaviour over IPv4 and IPv6 concurrently, with sessions on both
#   address families active at the same time and neither interfering with the other.
#   - Allowed users listed in the SSH server user configuration able to log in over IPv6.
#   - Denied or unlisted users rejected over IPv6, with the connection refused or closed and
#   no CLI access granted.
#   - Failed authentication attempts over IPv6 (wrong password, unknown user, disabled
#   account) rejected without partial access, with retry limits and lockout behaviour
#   applied as configured.
#   - Multiple concurrent IPv6 SSH sessions supported up to the configured maximum session
#   limit, each independent and correctly attributed to its own user.
#   - Connection attempts beyond the maximum session limit refused or queued per design,
#   with existing sessions unaffected.
#   - An administrator-initiated termination of a remote IPv6 SSH session resulting in the
#   targeted session closing immediately, its resources released, and other sessions
#   remaining intact.
#   - SSH server disabled state resulting in IPv6 connection attempts being refused with no
#   listener present on the SSH port for IPv6.
#   - Existing IPv6 sessions handled deterministically when the SSH server is disabled
#   (dropped per product design) without device instability.
#   - Re-enabling the SSH server restoring IPv6 connectivity and accepting new sessions
#   without a device restart.
#   - Unsupported or invalid conditions handled gracefully: malformed IPv6 addresses,
#   unreachable destinations, mismatched scope identifiers, unsupported ciphers/key exchange
#   algorithms, and unsupported protocol versions — each producing a clean rejection and no
#   crash, leak or lockup.
#   - Persistence of SSH server enable state, IPv6 listener and allow/deny user
#   configuration across configuration save and device restart.
#   - Operational status output showing SSH server state, listening address families, active
#   session list with source IPv6 addresses, usernames and session identifiers.
#   - Log entries recording IPv6 SSH session establishment, authentication success and
#   failure, administrative session termination, and server enable/disable transitions, each
#   with accurate timestamps and source IPv6 address.
#   - No unexpected errors, exception traces, memory leaks or resource exhaustion on the DUT
#   after repeated IPv6 SSH connect, authenticate, command execution and disconnect cycles.
# ==================================================================

